============================================================
  WHAT THIS TELLS US FOR THE HALF-PAGE (read against the evidence):
============================================================
  (a) p(x,t): the return distribution; best observed as it RELAXES
      after a shock, NOT as a static rolling window.
  (b) initial condition: the shocked (post-crash) return distribution.
  (c) t=0: the crash/shock date — the one real-market event that
      RESETS the system and gives a known start.
  D1 tests whether memory exists at all (slow autocorr decay).
  D2 tests whether post-crash relaxation is power-law (fractional)
     vs exponential (Markovian) — the core justification.
  D3/D4 test whether the transient is real and how noisy one window is.
  If D1+D2 show long memory and power-law relaxation, the post-shock
  observation model is defensible. If not, the honest conclusion is
  that real data lacks a usable transient — a valid, important finding.
